# RegulAIt deployment configuration — EXAMPLE
#
# You should not normally write this by hand. `scripts/install.sh` renders the
# real file (`<install-dir>/.env`) from flags/prompts, preserves every secret
# already in it on a re-run, and refuses a weak REGULAIT_DATA_KEY. This example
# exists so an operator can see the full surface in one place, and so a
# configuration-management tool has something to template from.
#
# NOTE ON THE FILENAME. The repository's .gitignore refuses `.env` and `.env.*`
# outright — a rule worth keeping, since the real file contains the key that
# decrypts every stored credential. This example therefore lives here, as
# `infra/deploy/env.example`, rather than as a root `.env.example` that the
# ignore rule would have swallowed.
#
# Copy to <install-dir>/.env only if you are deliberately bypassing the
# installer; otherwise run:
#   scripts/install.sh --mode byoc --domain regulait.acme.example --tls letsencrypt

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deployment identity
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hosted | byoc | air_gapped. INSTALLER-LEVEL METADATA ONLY — no gateway code
# reads this. ADR-0015's mode is a column on each deploy_targets row, set in the
# admin console. This line records what the host was installed as.
REGULAIT_DEPLOY_MODE=byoc
# Recorded in <install-dir>/.regulait-version and used by the update verifier's
# downgrade check.
REGULAIT_VERSION=0.1.0
COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=regulait
# The https origin this deployment is reached at (optionally with a base path).
# It is the ONLY origin links in outbound mail may use: the Outlook approval
# courier is refused while it is unset, and a set-but-invalid value refuses the
# gateway boot (http only for localhost/loopback). Not a secret. ADR-0121.
# REGULAIT_PUBLIC_URL=https://regulait.acme.example

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secrets — treat this entire file as a secret (chmod 600)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE ONE THAT MATTERS. 64 hex characters = 32 bytes, AES-256-GCM
# (apps/gateway/src/secrets.ts). Encrypts every stored connector token, model
# API key and TOTP secret. Generate with:  openssl rand -hex 32
#
# Lose it and a restore onto a new host recovers every row and decrypts NONE of
# the credentials, permanently. KEEP AN OUT-OF-BAND COPY — a password manager,
# an SSM Parameter Store SecureString under a DIFFERENT KMS key, an offline
# safe. Anywhere whose failure is independent of this host's disk.
REGULAIT_DATA_KEY=replace-me-with-openssl-rand-hex-32

# Authenticates as an admin with NO user identity (apps/gateway/src/auth.ts).
# Exists only to create the first real admin. Delete this line and restart the
# stack once you have done so.
REGULAIT_BOOTSTRAP_TOKEN=replace-me

# Postgres. Only honoured on FIRST initdb — once the pgdata volume exists,
# changing it locks the gateway out rather than rotating anything. Rotate with
# ALTER USER inside the running database, then update both lines together.
REGULAIT_DB_PASSWORD=replace-me
REGULAIT_DATABASE_URL=postgres://regulait:replace-me@db:5432/regulait

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Application
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1 loads the demo dataset (users dana/avery/admin, demo API keys printed once
# to the container log). Keep this 0 on any customer deployment.
SEED_DEMO=0

# ADR-0031. The ONLY peer allowed to speak for the client via X-Forwarded-*.
# req.ip lands on every auth_sessions row and is the attribution pillar 1
# sells, so this must name the proxy and nothing else. 172.28.0.2 is Caddy's
# pinned address on the compose network. If you terminate TLS on your own
# proxy, set this to that proxy's address — never to a range.
REGULAIT_TRUSTED_PROXIES=172.28.0.2

# OPTIONAL. HSTS is a browser-side, host-scoped, NON-REVOCABLE commitment.
# Unset  -> max-age=86400, no includeSubDomains, no preload (the gateway's
#           bounded default, apps/gateway/src/hsts.ts).
# "off"  -> no header at all.
# Raise it only once a stable real domain is in place. NOTE: leaving the
# variable UNSET is not the same as setting it to the empty string, which means
# "off" — which is why the installer passes it through a list-form compose
# override rather than a `${VAR:-}` default.
#REGULAIT_HSTS=max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TLS (ADR-0029) — Caddy terminates in front of the gateway
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The hostname Caddy serves (and, for Let's Encrypt, issues for).
REGULAIT_TLS_HOST=regulait.acme.example
# EMPTY        -> Caddy's automatic HTTPS: real Let's Encrypt. Requires
#                 outbound ACME and an inbound HTTP-01 challenge on :80.
# tls internal -> Caddy's own local CA. Zero outbound. The air-gapped choice.
#                 Distribute the root cert to clients:
#                 docker compose -p regulait cp \
#                   caddy:/data/caddy/pki/authorities/local/root.crt ./root.crt
REGULAIT_TLS_ISSUER=
# Optional ACME account contact, e.g. "email ops@acme.example". Let's Encrypt
# accepts an account with no contact, so empty is fine.
REGULAIT_TLS_EMAIL_DIRECTIVE=
REGULAIT_TLS_UPSTREAM=gateway:3000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Identity — INFORMATIONAL ONLY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# No gateway code reads this. OIDC providers are database rows created through
# the admin API, and ADR-0043 puts the issuer URL behind the egress guard, so
# the issuer's host needs an egress_allow_hosts entry BEFORE discovery will
# run. Recorded here so an operator can see what this box was installed
# against; the installer prints the exact two API calls in its summary.
REGULAIT_OIDC_ISSUER=

# OPTIONAL, ADR-0036 (SAML 2.0 SSO). Both are bounded.
#  - ENTITY_ID pins the deployment-global SP entity id. Unset, it is derived
#    from the request host. Pin it before handing metadata to an IdP admin if
#    anything in front of the gateway can rename the host — every IdP's
#    AudienceRestriction is written against this exact string.
#  - CLOCK_SKEW_MINUTES widens NotBefore/NotOnOrAfter tolerance. Default 2,
#    CLAMPED to 9. There is no "unlimited".
#REGULAIT_SAML_ENTITY_ID=https://regulait.acme.example/auth/saml/metadata
#REGULAIT_SAML_CLOCK_SKEW_MINUTES=2

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Model providers — OPTIONAL, and read at dispatch time only
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Setting one of these activates real model dispatch platform-wide with no
# admin-UI paste and no key stored in the database. A stored per-user or
# platform credential still takes precedence.
#
# READ docs/deployment/DATA_BOUNDARY.md §4 BEFORE SETTING ANY OF THESE ON AN
# AIR-GAPPED DEPLOYMENT: a built-in provider with no baseUrl override is NOT
# behind the egress guard, so an invocation will attempt the vendor's public
# API and the network is the only thing stopping it. The air-gapped-native path
# is a custom provider (ADR-0034) pointed at your own vLLM/Ollama endpoint,
# which IS guarded.
#ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
#ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=
#OPENAI_API_KEY=
#OPENAI_BASE_URL=
#GOOGLE_API_KEY=
#GEMINI_API_KEY=
#XAI_API_KEY=
